@prefix device: <http://example.org/orexis/device#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/device#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .

<http://example.org/orexis/device> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Device" ;
    rdfs:comment """What a thing is MADE OF — one word, for the inventory and the wiring.

A knowledge-only package: no Python, no capability, nothing to grant. The kernel does not own it
(the-substrate-is-not-the-minds) and neither does any capability: **nothing that observes or acts
requires it.** A sensor is a sosa:Sensor and an actuator is a sosa:Actuator, whether or not
anybody built one (a-stand-in-is-not-a-device).

**The axis is SUBSTRATE, and it is not SOSA's.** SOSA's classes are functional and the spec is
explicit that any of them may be virtual: `sosa:Sensor` says *this observes*, never *this exists*.
`device:Device` says a physical node is there — with legs, a bus and an address — which is the
claim `mc:hasPin` and `mc:logicVolts` need as their domain, because boards have pins. That is what
it is FOR: an inventory of parts and a harness joining them. Nothing else should ask.

So it is stated by INSTANCES and by the classes that are physical by definition — a board, a
peripheral. A class naming a role does not state it, because the role is available to a thing that
was never built.""" .

:Device a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Device" ;
    rdfs:comment "A physical node: mechanism, never policy. What has legs, a place in the inventory and a line in the harness." .
